---
import Project from "../components/project.astro";
import "../css.css";
import { Font } from "astro:assets";

const falafelAlt = "a 3d render of a piece of falafel spinning around, orbited by a ring of sesame seeds and other ambiguous falafel wrap ingredients";
const mythAlt = "a photograph of a photobook, open to a spread with two images. The left image depicts some colorful stairs, and the right image depicts a colorful outdoor swimming pool";
---

<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <meta charset="utf-8" />
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
        <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
        <title>DP→ITP</title>
        <meta name="title" content="DP→ITP" />
        <meta name="description" content="duncan’s portfolio for itp at nyu" />
        <Font cssVariable="--vevey" preload />
        <Font cssVariable="--atk" preload />
    </head>
    <body>
        <header>
            <h1 aria-label="Duncan Petrie is applying to the Interactive Telecommunications Program" tabindex="0">
                <span>
                    <span>D</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 5.3; --color: green;">uncan</span>
                    <span>P</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 18; --color: blue;">etrie <span style="--color: tomato;">is</span> <span style="--color: cadetblue;">applying</span> <span style="--color: red;">to</span> <span style="--color: orange;">the</span></span>
                </span>
                <span>
                    →
                </span>
                <span>
                    <span>I</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 8.5; --color: cadetblue;">nteractive</span>
                    <span>T</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 16; margin-left: -0.125em; --color: orange;">elecommunications</span>
                    <span>P</span>
                    <span style="--chars: 6; --color: blue;">rogram</span>
                </span>
            </h1>
        </header>
        <main>
            <Project title="Spiders on the Web" star year="2026" tags={["speaking", "website"]} preview="arachnophobia.webp">
                <p class="half">
                    In 2026, I was invited to speak at <a href="https://webdevconf.com/events/2026/" target="_blank" title="Web Dev Conf 2026">Web Dev Conf</a>, an indie tech conference in Bristol, UK, about building whimsical browser animations from scratch. We learned about frame-based animation, spring physics, and faberge eggs.
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/talk.webp" alt="duncan giving a talk at web dev conf in bristol, october 2026" />
                    <figcaption>Live demo 😬 at WDC.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    The talk culminated in <a href="https://spiders.duncanpetrie.com" target="_blank" title="https://spiders.duncanpetrie.com">spiders.duncanpetrie.com</a>, a little playground where you can be a spider. The site is multiplayer, so bring your friends! If you get close enough together, you’ll start kissing (this is not a joke).
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    I also built a <a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/a-spider-on-the-web/jnlhooedjlgccjkicbbolgbojlaigkle?authuser=0&hl=en" target="_blank" title="a spider on the web - chrome web store">browser extension</a>, which lets you take your spider on the go. It also periodically makes spiders run across your screen. This is a fun addition to the web browsing experience, and does not activate your fight-or-flight instincts.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    See <a href="https://wdc.duncanpetrie.com" target="_blank" title="https://wdc.duncanpetrie.com">wdc.duncanpetrie.com</a> for slides and more details. A recording is available on <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=88_qqxNvZmQ" target="_blank" title="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=88_qqxNvZmQ">YouTube</a>.
                </p>
                <br/>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/spider-cursor.webp" alt="a little spider following a mouse cursor around on wikipedia" />
                    <figcaption>It’s nice to have company.</figcaption>
                </figure>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Planet Falafel" star year="2026" tags={["microblog", "essay"]} preview="planet-falafel.webp" previewNoBorder>
                <figure class="hal">
                    <img src="/images/falafel.png" alt="screenshot of the landing page of falafel.fyi" draggable="false" />
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    <a href="https://falafel.fyi" target="_blank" title="falafel.fyi">https://falafel.fyi</a> is a microblog about my favorite kind of ball. Every time I order a falafel wrap from a new cart, cafe, or restaurant, I write a little review, and put it there. It also has an <a href="https://falafel.fyi/about" target="_blank" title="falafel.fyi/about">essay</a> about the origins of falafel, in case you’re hungry for history.
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/falafel-map.webp" alt="screenshot of the map on falafel.fyi" draggable="false" />
                    <figcaption style="margin-top: -2%;">A screenshot of the map on <a href="https://falafel.fyi" target="_blank" title="falafel.fyi">falafel.fyi</a>.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    This project was self-directed. I used an internal api to sync my website with my list of saved falafel shops on Google Maps, researched and wrote all the content, learned Blender to model and animate a piece of falafel, drew various icons/assets with a drawing tablet, used Figma to design the website, and Astro to build it. The goal was to get better at doing these kinds of projects, so I used AI as little as possible, and only as a learning tool.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Pedigree" year="2026" tags="story" preview="dog-pool.jpg">
                <blockquote class="half">
                    Johnny was always a bad husband, but I didn’t know it at first. I’d never hung off someone’s arm like that. Strangers gave me flowers, and I had to wear sunglasses indoors, because the paparazzi flashes gave me headaches.
                </blockquote>
                <p class="half">
                    In August, I won a <a href="https://www.quarter--mile.com/1picture1000words/original" title="https://www.quarter--mile.com/1picture1000words" target="_blank">short story competition</a> with a story about show dog adultery. The prompt was to write exactly 1000 words about this incredibly boring picture:
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/dog-pool.jpg" alt="a huge fawn mastiff sits upright, like a person, on a dark wicker patio sofa, looking out at a turquoise swimming pool" draggable="false" />
                    <figcaption>
                        Is a picture truly worth a thousand words? Is <em>this</em> picture worth a thousand words?
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    You can read the full story <a href="https://www.quarter--mile.com/1picture1000words/winners/pedigree" target="_blank" title="pedigree - 1 picture 1000 words">here</a>.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Sketchbooks" year="2026 → ?" tags="doodles" preview="ds.webp">
                <p class="half">
                    I have a daily drawing practice. Mostly, I use a fine-liner and draw in tiny boxes. I’m a perfectionist, and drawing small makes it easier for me to experiment; if I make a mistake, there’s space to try again!
                </p>
                <figure>
                    <div class="img-row" style="--img-width: 40%">
                        <img src="/images/d1.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d2.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d3.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d4.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d5.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d6.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d7.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                        <img src="/images/d8.webp" alt="little drawings in a little notebook" />
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>Some tiny drawings in a tiny notebook.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    I also draw on a tablet. It’s not as nice as drawing on paper, but very useful for making website assets. Some examples are the scroll tracker for my <a href="https://falafel.fyi/about" target="_blank" title="falafel.fyi/about">essay about falafel</a> and the <a href="https://wdc.duncanpetrie.com/playground/spring-chicken/" target="_blank" title="wdc.duncanpetrie.com/playground/spring-chicken">spring chicken demo</a> for my recent talk.
                </p>
                <p class="half">Some digital doodles:</p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/ipad.webp" alt="animated drawings done on a tablet" />
                    <figcaption>A collection of barely-animated drawings done done on a tablet.</figcaption>
                </figure>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Pigeon-Fill the Sky" star year="2025" tags={["photobook", "website"]} preview="pigeon-glitch.webp">
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/flip-pigeon-2.webp" alt="a looping gif of somebody flipping through the pigeon-fill the sky (the book)" />
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    <em>To Pigeon-Fill the Sky</em> is a photographic storybook about AI, the creative process, and, of course, pigeons. I created the photobook in 2024, and the web-based version in 2025, for <em>The Wrong Biennale</em>.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    If you have time, I’d recommend you check out the website itself for a better idea of this project! It's a ~5 minute scroll-based narrative.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    ✴︎ <a href="https://pigeons.duncanpetrie.com/website" target="_blank" title="https://pigeons.duncanpetrie.com/website">https://pigeons.duncanpetrie.com/website</a>
                </p>
                <p class="half">        
                    The site’s <a href="https://pigeons.duncanpetrie.com/about" title="https://pigeons.duncanpetrie.com/about" target="_blank">about</a> page includes more information about my process, including how I used AI, how I built the site, and how I thought about adapting a photobook for the web.
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/bigempty.webp" alt="a photograph of a big empty sky with part of a tree in the bottom right corner" />
                    <figcaption>A digital photograph of a big empty sky.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    In 2024, I returned to photography after a long break, and my new pictures were bad. They were boring. I wasn’t being patient. I wasn’t waiting for decisive moments. There were no birds in the skies.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    Everyone was talking about AI at the time (as opposed to now, of course), and I thought of an artist who might eschew patience and hard work for these tools, who might add the decisive moment in post.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    So I told the story of that artist. <em>To Pigeon-Fill the Sky</em> is a collection of analog photographs, digital photographs, real pigeons, and generated pigeons. It’s a sort of descent into madness.
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/pigeon-chimney.webp" alt="artificially generated pigeons superimposed over a photograph of a chimney and an empty blue sky" />
                    <figcaption>Pigeons across the empty sky. I had a lot of trouble generating pigeons; this file required over a hundred Generative Fill layers.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    I used Photoshop’s Generative Fill tool for this. I had very little creative control over the output. When I asked for a bunch of pigeons, for example, I got one. When I asked for a hundred, I got three. Each image became a hodgepodge of generations, all jostling for space.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    At a certain point, Photoshop started to break. Too many layers? Too much Generative Fill? Collages of visual artifacts filled the screen, much more beautiful than anything Photoshop had generated. They wouldn’t export, so I had to take screenshots. I had found, in the end, a sort of decisive moment.
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/pigeon-glitch.webp" alt="gif showing various photoshop glitches" />
                    <figcaption>Screenshots of various glitches in Photoshop.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    I named all the pigeons I generated. I think there's something interesting in this inversion of labor: I used AI to generate disfigured, incongruous pigeons, when I easily could have waited for a real pigeon to fly in front of my lens, and I spent hours coming up with names myself, when an LLM could have done that in seconds. What is the value of labor? Has its value changed, now that AI has made it so easy to <em>not</em> labor?
                </p>
                <figure>
                    <img src="/images/names.webp" alt="a looping screen recording of to pigeon-fill the sky (the website), where names are appearing over generated pigeons" />
                    <figcaption style="margin-top: -5%;">Each artificial pigeon has a name.</figcaption>
                </figure>
