                <p class="half">
                    Again, if you have time, I’d recommend you check out the website itself, <a href="https://pigeons.duncanpetrie.com/website" target="_blank" title="https://pigeons.duncanpetrie.com/website">pigeons.duncanpetrie.com</a>, for a better idea of this project! The site’s <em>about</em> page includes more detail about my process, including how I built the site, and how I thought about translating a book into digital.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Palimpsest" year="2024 → ?" tags="photography" preview="flip-palimpsest-2.webp">
                <figure>
                    <div class="img-row" style="--img-width: 40%">
                        <img src="/images/palimpsest-spread.webp" alt="spread of a zine of colorful photographs" />
                        <img src="/images/palimpsest-spread-1.webp" alt="spread of a zine of colorful photographs" />
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>Prints, captions, and a spread in a zine of photographs from <em>Palimpsest</em>.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    <em>Palimpsest</em> is an ongoing photographic project exploring nature in the human landscape, and what the world might look like when we are gone.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    I’m interested in the idea of “posthistory,” which is the opposite of “prehistory.” The latter refers to a time before recorded history; it stands to reason, as the world burns, that there might be an <em>after</em>, as well. That we may be overwritten.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    For a long time, I’ve wished my work was more socially engaged, more directly related to the issues I care about, and this project is an attempt at that. I also want to include more writing in my photography projects, so I’m experimenting with captions, weaving a poem or narrative alongside the images. I’m still refining this, but hopefully it gives some idea of my practice.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    See more at <a href="https://duncanpetrie.com/palimpsest" title="https://duncanpetrie.com/palimpsest" target="_blank">duncanpetrie.com/palimpsest</a>.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Wikiπdia" year="2025" tags={["website", ]} preview="wikipi.webp">
                <p class="half">
                    How do you fit one infinity into another? <a href="https://wikipi.duncanpetrie.com/" target="_blank" title="https://wikipi.duncanpetrie.com">Wikiπdia</a> is a very simple website that uses π as an index for Wikipedia articles with numbers in their titles. It’s mostly asteroids.
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/wikipi.webp" alt="screen recording of wikiπdia, where the digits of π link to wikipedia articles with numbers in their titles" draggable="false" />
                    <figcaption>
                        The digits of π link to wikipedia articles with those numbers in their titles.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Lingermyth" year="2022" tags="photobook" preview="flip-myth-3.webp">
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/myth-covers.webp" alt="photograph of two copies of the same book, laying next to each other. one is showing the front cover, one is showing the back cover" />
                    <figcaption>The front and back covers of <em>Lingermyth</em>.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    My final project for my BA was a book of 50 photographs called <em>Lingermyth</em>. It was an attempt to put the theory in <em>On Yearning</em> (see below) into practice. It was my first photobook, and I’d definitely change some things about it, but I’m still really proud of the photography!
                </p>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/myth-spread.webp" alt="photograph of a spread of a photobook with two colorful images" />
                    <figcaption>A spread in <em>Lingermyth</em>.</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    From the opening text:
                </p>
                <blockquote class="half">
                    Gradually, we grew out of our past, explained away halos and beasts and cities of clouds. History is written by those that live in the present. But it’s still there, that old world. Beneath our own, like a first coat of paint.
                </blockquote>
                <blockquote class="half">
                    This book is a collection of those echoes. It depicts a world removed from our own, a world glimpsed in reflections and walls, in old paint and houseplants and long shadows on the ground.
                </blockquote>
                <p class="half">
                    <em>Lingermyth</em> was recognized in the EZIO Color Awards, the AOP Student Awards, and the 2022 <a href="https://issuu.com/anartistsinfo/docs/a-n_degree_shows_guide_2022/10" title="a-n Degree Shows Guide 2022" target="_blank">a-n Degree Shows Guide</a>.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    You can see the full pdf at <a href="https://duncanpetrie.com/myth.pdf" target="_blank" title="duncanpetrie.com/myth.pdf">duncanpetrie.com/myth.pdf</a>.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="On Yearning" year="2021" tags="dissertation" preview="diss-pages.webp" previewNoBorder>
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/yearn.jpg" alt="photograph of a gull flying across the lonely sky" draggable="false" />
                </figure>
                <blockquote class="half">
                    I could not have said where “over there” was. I was on the edge of the world, or so it felt, and I wanted to see beyond. It was a sort of nostalgia, but not for any real past. A nostalgia for the future, maybe, the future that can no longer be.
                </blockquote>
                <p class="half">
                    I wrote my undergraduate dissertation, <em>On Yearning,</em> about the feeling I get when I look to the horizon and long to see beyond it. It explores art as a form of worldbuilding, and it’s the foundation of my photographic practice.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    It is available here: <a href="https://duncanpetrie.com/yearning.pdf" target="_blank" title="on yearning">duncanpetrie.com/yearning.pdf</a>.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="The Commons" year="2021" tags={["short film", ]} preview="wiki.gif">
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/wiki.gif" style="width: 100%;" alt="gif of 24 dutch children trying to fit inside a phone booth" draggable="false" />
                    <figcaption>
                        24 dutch children try to fit inside a phone booth. This is just one of the many great things you can find on Wikimedia Commons.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    The full breadth of humanity, every hope and every sorrow, every song and every sigh, is on <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Main_Page" target="_blank" title="commons.wikimedia.org">Wikimedia Commons</a>, an open, public file repository maintained by the Wikimedia Foundation.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    In Spring of 2021, during a Covid lockdown, I created a film about the Commons using 144 of those files. It won first prize for moving image in the <a href="https://www.aopawards.com/student-awards-2021/" target="_blank" title="https://www.aopawards.com/student-awards-2021/">AOP student awards</a>. You can watch it on YouTube <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JRXZAaDxGCQ/" target="_blank" title="youtube: a triumph of the commons">here</a>.
                </p>
                <!-- <iframe class="half" style="aspect-ratio: 16/9; margin-block-end: 4rem;" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/JRXZAaDxGCQ?si=Y26oNIntLkXSUN8b" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe> -->
            </Project>
            <Project title="Synesthesim" tags="program" year="2020" preview="syn.gif">
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/syn.gif" style="width: 100%;" alt="gif of 24 dutch children trying to fit inside a phone booth" draggable="false" />
                    <figcaption>
                        Here, I’m typing my computer password.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    I have <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grapheme–color_synesthesia" target="_blank" title="wikipedia: grapheme-color synesthesia">grapheme-color synesthesia</a>, which means that my brain forms strong associations between colors and letters. During the first summer of Covid, I plotted the colors and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synesthesia#Spatial_sequence_synesthesia" target="_blank" title="wikipedia: spatial sequence synesthesia">spacial sequences</a> of my alphabet, and made a little program which listens for key presses and displays bursts of color.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    See the project on <a href="https://github.com/probablyduncan/synesthesim" target="_blank" title="github: synesthesim">github</a>.
                </p>
            </Project>
            <Project title="Pizza, Fast!" tags="short film" year="2020" preview="pizza.gif">
                <figure class="half">
                    <img src="/images/pizza.gif" style="width: 100%;" alt="timelapse gif of pizza rising in an oven" draggable="false" />
                </figure>
                <p class="half">
                    In March of 2020, I left university and moved back into my parents’ house. During that first lockdown, I couldn't really go outside. My family did a lot of baking. For a moving image assignment, I created a short timelapse film from almost 2,500 individual photographs, documenting the making of pizza. An artifact of Covid, I guess.
                </p>
                <p class="half">
                    You can watch it on Youtube <a href="https://youtu.be/-sBHX07eIMY/" target="_blank" title="youtube: 
i made pizza, really fast">here</a>.
                </p>
            </Project>
